          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Jonas Salk's Early Life</strong><br>\nJonas Salk was born on October 28, 1914, in New York City. He was the son of Russian-Jewish immigrants who valued education, although they themselves had little formal schooling. His intelligence and hard work led him to earn a medical degree from New York University. Early in his career, Salk chose to pursue medical research instead of becoming a practicing physician, showing a keen interest in contributing to the wider field of medical science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Polio Epidemic</strong><br>\nDuring the early 20th century, polio epidemics were a global crisis, causing paralysis and death across the world. Polio mainly affected children, which led to it being known as \"infantile paralysis.\" The fear of polio was widespread, and the summer outbreaks often forced communities into quarantine, with parents keeping their children at home to avoid infection.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of the Vaccine</strong><br>\nJonas Salk began working on a polio vaccine in the late 1940s. At that time, there were no effective treatments for the disease. Salk believed that a killed-virus vaccine, which uses inactivated polio virus, could immunize individuals without the risk of infecting them. He conducted early tests on patients who had already contracted polio and then on healthy volunteers, including himself and his family.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
