      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Salk Vaccine Trials</strong><br>\nIn 1954, one of the largest medical experiments in history took place: the field trial of Salk's polio vaccine. It involved 1.8 million children across the United States, Canada, and Finland. The results, announced in 1955, showed that the vaccine was safe and effective, leading to a massive public health campaign to vaccinate children.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Reaction to the Vaccine</strong><br>\nThe public hailed Jonas Salk as a hero when the success of his vaccine was announced. Pictures of Salk administering the vaccine to children were published around the world, and he became an international celebrity. The vaccine quickly led to a dramatic decrease in polio cases.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Global Health</strong><br>\nThe polio vaccine developed by Salk had a profound impact on global health. By the end of the 20th century, polio had been eliminated in the Americas, and cases worldwide had decreased by over 99%. Salk's work laid the foundation for other inactivated vaccines and contributed to the broader field of virology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
