          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Career and Contributions</strong><br>\nAfter the polio vaccine, Salk founded the Salk Institute for Biological Studies in La Jolla, California, in 1963. This institute became a leading center for medical and scientific research, attracting many renowned scientists. Salk continued to research and publish on various topics, including multiple sclerosis and AIDS, until his death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jonas Salk's Philosophy</strong><br>\nSalk was unique among scientists in that he refused to patent the polio vaccine, stating that the vaccine belonged to the people and that to patent it would be like patenting the sun. His attitude towards scientific discovery as a public service rather than a personal profit-making endeavor was emblematic of his humanitarian approach.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nJonas Salk died on June 23, 1995, leaving behind a legacy as the man who developed the vaccine that conquered polio. He was not only remembered for his scientific achievements but also for his commitment to humanitarian principles in science. His work continues to inspire scientists and public health professionals to this day.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
